<h2>Older homes, big trees, and common claims</h2>
<p>Many Alamo Heights homes are older than the surrounding suburbs, with a mix of pier-and-beam and slab foundations, wood siding, stucco, stone, and brick, and roofs ranging from composition shingle to tile and metal. Older materials and construction methods can complicate a claim in a few ways:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Discontinued roofing, siding, or windows may be hard to match, which can raise questions about how much must be replaced.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades.</strong> Repairs to older homes are more likely to trigger current code requirements, which are usually paid only under ordinance or law coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Tree impact.</strong> When wind drops a mature oak or pecan on a house, the claim may involve the roof, framing, interior water damage, and debris removal, each with its own policy terms.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> Insurers may apply heavy depreciation to older roofs and finishes. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</li>
</ul>
